 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6059+) **War preparations now take priority over Barbarian expeditions:** Base AdvCiv/K-Mod treated the period before a planned attack became ready as if no rival war were being prepared. For example, in save file 450, BBAI logging showed Hannibal preparing to attack nearby Willem, whose power was only 54% of Hannibal's, while 7 of Hannibal's units were still being managed for an expedition to the Barbarian city Scythian 22 tiles away instead of building enough strength for the much more profitable rival war. To fix this, the AI now uses normal war readiness as soon as it has any real war plan. In the confirming run, the affected groups of 6-9 units did not leave for distant Barbarian cities. Instead, they continued assembling enough strength for the planned rival war and remained nearby in case they were needed for defense. See [KI#188.4](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1884---fixed-base-advcivk-mod-bug-ai-armies-preparing-war-against-a-civilization-could-still-be-classified-as-hunting-only-barbarian-cities).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6059+) **AIs no longer do charity Barbarian cleanup for their rivals:** Following the readiness fix above, BBAI logging exposed a broader problem: a fully prepared army could still spend many turns attacking a distant Barbarian city that it would not benefit from and ultimately raze. For example, in save file 450, BBAI logging showed that Hannibal mobilized 3 units on turn 74 against the Barbarian city Libyan, 16 tiles away. Reaching and capturing it took 16 turns, and Hannibal then razed it. He gained no city and only some conquest gold, but lost troops and time that could have supported offense or defense. Shaka, the only nearby player likely to keep the city, gained much more: he founded Bulawayo on the exact plot only 10 turns later without having to capture Libyan himself. With the nearby Barbarian pressure removed, Shaka could also focus on his economy or build up his army without losing units to Barbarian attacks or becoming easier prey for other rivals, and he eventually won this run by Space. To fix this, the AI now avoids trips longer than the new XML-tunable limit of 3 path turns (to preserve quick tactical raids even when the city is not worth keeping) unless `AI_isSASCityLikelyToBenefitUsLongTerm` says the city is worth keeping (as of now, generally because it is close enough and has enough useful land and food after bonuses are considered). In the confirming run, Hannibal did not send his units to clear those distant Barbarian cities around turn 74. Instead, he kept them available for potentially better attacks or defense. Much later, between turns 168 and 195, he captured 5 Arabian cities, which now made capturing and not razing Libyan and Scythian now worth it as they became close enough (and are otherwise valuable enough to keep), which he did on turns 204 and 208. As for Shaka, he spent his own early military effort fighting near Libyan but failed to capture it. He later became Hannibal's vassal, and Hannibal won by Space. See [KI#188.5](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1885---improved-ai-armies-could-waste-their-strength-razing-distant-barbarian-cities-instead-of-leaving-barbarian-pressure-to-weaken-nearer-rivals-and-remaining-available-for-offense-or-defense).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6049+) **Offensive armies can interrupt or divide an expedition to defend nearby endangered cities:** Following KI#188, similar logging showed a ready AI army, including one already in enemy territory after being backstabbed, could in Base AdvCiv/K-Mod select another enemy city while an underdefended own city was reachable within a few turns. The clearest diagnostic case was in save file 450: Baghdad needed 12 defenders and had 5, but a healthy 11-unit Arabian city-assault army only two turns away waited for more units, grew to 23 units / 32% of Arabia's military, and attacked Prague; Baghdad fell, Prague was not captured, and Arabia retook Baghdad five turns later after its population had fallen from 10 to 4. To fix and enhance this, before choosing its next offensive target, the army now first tries to counter a suitable nearby invader when its area is considered defensive. If no such counterattack is selected, it can send enough units to fill an endangered city's remaining defender shortage within three turns and continue the expedition with the rest. The AI does not begin a rescue when a one-unit group cannot fill a larger shortage or when filling it would consume more than three quarters of the group, and doomed-city evacuation still takes priority. This lets a military advantage serve defense without automatically abandoning every viable offensive. After the fix, history diverged, but a similar save file 450 run showed the intended choices working: an Arabian city-assault group suspended its expedition to counter Holy Roman units near Baghdad, which remained Arabian. Later, a Dutch army sent 10 of its 19 units to protect Amsterdam while keeping 9 in the field; the Dutch won three of four nearby land battles and successfully retained the city. Overall, 11 of the 16 expedition-split decisions targeted cities that survived the immediate war or remained held through the test. The five failures were all on the collapsing Holy Roman front, where Najran, Vienna, Nuremberg, Prague, and Aachen still fell, so the response improves viable defenses without pretending an overwhelmed empire can save every city. See [KI#188.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1882---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-issue-offensive-armies-could-ignore-nearby-endangered-cities-instead-of-going-back-to-try-to-help-defending-them).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6049+) **Emergency city reinforcements now complete their assigned rescue:** Similar logging showed that the first prototypes of the cure above could detach defenders only for them to immediately regroup with their offensive army instead of reaching the endangered city. For example, at Chichen Itza, 6 units detached from an 8-unit army, regrouped, and had to be detached again two turns later; the city fell before the rescue was completed. This was not an inherited Base AdvCiv/K-Mod issue, but a missing part of our candidate cure. To complete the cure, emergency reinforcements now retain their city assignment, count as incoming defenders so the AI does not duplicate the same response, and remain in the city while needed; once the danger or defender shortage ends, they return to normal duties. In the confirming save file 450 run, 7 units successfully reached Amsterdam while the other 11 remained together for their campaign. See [KI#188.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1882---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-issue-offensive-armies-could-ignore-nearby-endangered-cities-instead-of-going-back-to-try-to-help-defending-them).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6050+) **Large AI armies parked with nothing reachable to attack:** BBAI logging showed a major Base AdvCiv/K-Mod bug: even while at peace, with no planned war and no rival or Barbarian city it could reach, the AI could keep waiting for reinforcements and effectively lock more units into the same army so they could not be released for other duties. For example, in save file 450, a United States group grew from 6 units on T126 to 18 of the empire's 47 military units by T225, and then, after the short war, it returned to peace still holding 21 of 51 units on T247. So it concentrated about 40% of the AI's military in one city and left far fewer units available to defend the rest of its empire or pursue another opportunity, which was very problematic. To fix this, when the AI has nothing to attack, it now stops waiting for more reinforcements: if the army is already in its own territory and not immediately threatened, the AI now only keeps one modest core group ready for a future city attack. As for the excess units, they now instead leave the group and resume defending cities or preparing another expedition. Note: an army that is still abroad or in immediate danger remains together until it returns to its own territory and is no longer immediately threatened, so the fix does not break it apart while exposed. Note 2: the units can also regroup into a large army as soon as a real target or planned war appears. After the fix, confirming runs of save files 450 and 452 showed the intended behavior: in save file 452, Japan reduced its peacetime city-assault group with nothing to attack from 21 to 11 units, freeing 10 units for other duties; at the same time, the AI could still assemble large armies when a real war began, as Carthage attacked Susa with 63 units in save file 450, and China attacked Aksum with roughly 180-202 units in save file 452. See [KI#188.3](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1883---fixedimproved-major-base-advcivk-mod-bug-city-assault-armies-without-a-reachable-target-or-war-plan-could-keep-waiting-for-reinforcements-indefinitely-and-park-40-53-of-an-ais-military).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6068+) **Targetless armies no longer remain parked by gathering several smaller groups in the same city:** some time later, BBAI army diagnostics and SASGameSummary city snapshots showed another form of the inherited Base AdvCiv/K-Mod failure that the first cure above did not cover: in save file 450, Holy Rome was at peace and parked 71 of its 105 military units (68%) in Mainz across 12 groups, although no group exceeded the individual limit of 9. This was especially harmful because Mainz was only population 9 with 14 production and only 2 of its 6 worked plots improved (so rather low value), while stronger cities were left with small garrisons: population-17 Aachen capital produced 48 production and 53 commerce but held only 4 military units, and population-15 Nuremberg produced 57 production but also held only 4. To complete the cure, the retained limit now covers all city-assault-led groups on the same safe owned plot and prevents released units from immediately joining them again; defenders and general attackers outside those groups are unaffected. This reduced repeated release events from 591 to 78 and detachments of units already assigned to other military roles from 1,746 to 331. In the confirming run, Mainz had only 7 military units on turn 190 instead of 71, while genuine war plans still assembled armies up to 47 units. Save file 449 independently remained restrained with only 44 release events and 108 detachments, and still assembled a 57-unit wartime army. See [KI#188.3.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#18832---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-targetless-army-parking-across-several-groups-could-concentrate-71-units-68-of-an-ais-military-in-low-value-mainz-while-much-stronger-aachen-and-nuremberg-held-only-4-each).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6068+) **Targetless armies no longer remain parked by gathering several smaller groups in the same city:** some time later, BBAI army diagnostics and SASGameRecord city snapshots showed another form of the inherited Base AdvCiv/K-Mod failure that the first cure above did not cover: in save file 450, Holy Rome was at peace and parked 71 of its 105 military units (68%) in Mainz across 12 groups, although no group exceeded the individual limit of 9. This was especially harmful because Mainz was only population 9 with 14 production and only 2 of its 6 worked plots improved (so rather low value), while stronger cities were left with small garrisons: population-17 Aachen capital produced 48 production and 53 commerce but held only 4 military units, and population-15 Nuremberg produced 57 production but also held only 4. To complete the cure, the retained limit now covers all city-assault-led groups on the same safe owned plot and prevents released units from immediately joining them again; defenders and general attackers outside those groups are unaffected. This reduced repeated release events from 591 to 78 and detachments of units already assigned to other military roles from 1,746 to 331. In the confirming run, Mainz had only 7 military units on turn 190 instead of 71, while genuine war plans still assembled armies up to 47 units. Save file 449 independently remained restrained with only 44 release events and 108 detachments, and still assembled a 57-unit wartime army. See [KI#188.3.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#18832---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-targetless-army-parking-across-several-groups-could-concentrate-71-units-68-of-an-ais-military-in-low-value-mainz-while-much-stronger-aachen-and-nuremberg-held-only-4-each).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6069+) **Units no longer gather and wait in upgrade cities for upgrades the AI will not buy:** Base AdvCiv/K-Mod sent units to technically valid upgrade cities without checking whether the AI could afford the upgrade. AdvCiv-SAS later added economic checks that can also reject an affordable but poor-value upgrade, but the inherited travel-and-wait decision ignored both results. As a result, the Base issue remained and the new SAS mismatch made it substantially worse. For example, in save file 450, BBAI logging recorded 1,081 candidate checks and 1,053 waits in already crowded upgrade cities: 372 lacked enough gold and directly exposed the Base AdvCiv inherited affordability omission, another 631 had enough gold but failed the AdvCiv-SAS economic checks, and only 78 were currently eligible. This repeatedly tied up units for upgrades the AI would not buy. For up to 63 turns, the logs repeatedly recorded the same units being sent toward those cities or forced to skip their turns once there (`MISSION_SKIP`), so those units could not attack, reinforce another city, reposition for defense, or perform any other military duty on those turns, and only 53 of the 99 affected units were eventually upgraded. To fix this, the AI now checks the exact upgrade it intends to buy before travelling or waiting; if it cannot or will not buy it, the unit resumes its normal military role until the upgrade becomes genuinely available. After the fix, crowded-city waits fell from 1,053 to 9 in the confirming run, involving only 5 units that were all upgraded shortly afterward. Save file 449 independently confirmed that every unit waiting before its final turn was upgraded soon afterward; its only unresolved waits occurred immediately before another player won the game. See [KI#188.3.3](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#18833---fixedimproved-base-advcivk-mod-issue-upgrade-city-routing-ignored-affordability-and-helped-concentrate-up-to-72-of-an-ais-military-in-one-city-while-units-waited-up-to-63-turns-aggravated-by-its-mismatch-with-advciv-sas-economic-return-gates).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6051+) **AI war preparations no longer repeatedly gamble away their own mobilization:** BBAI logging in save file 452 showed a major UWAI issue: across all AIs, 152 war preparations began, 130 were canceled or redirected to another target, and only 17 became wars. Whenever UWAI reviewed a preparation whose war value had become negative, it converted how doubtful the plan looked into a chance to abandon it and rolled that chance on every negative review. China alone began 36 preparations: repeated abandonment rolls canceled 19, separate target-switch rolls redirected another 13, and only 2 became wars. Established preparations also used a random roll to decide when to become direct war. This could waste the military advantage and upkeep already committed to mobilization: 32 of 74 cancellations even selected the exact same target again later in the same team turn. To fix this, the AI now keeps a newly chosen plan through its first two reviews unless a hard failure, such as an illegal target or expired unprofitable preparation, requires immediate cancellation. After that, it cancels only when the personality- and game-speed-adjusted evidence reaches the tunable maximum for a clearly bad plan. It switches only when another rival is clearly better, uses a stable readiness threshold before beginning direct war, and cannot cancel and recreate the same target during one team turn; other targets remain available throughout. In the final confirming save-file 452 run, cancellations fell from 74 to 34, target switches from 22 to 15, same-turn reselections from 32 to 0, and reselections within three turns from 41 to 5, while actual wars increased from 22 to 29. Save file 450 independently confirmed no same-turn reselection, 39 preparations advancing to direct war, and 35 wars starting. See [KI#189](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#189---fixedimproved-major-uwai-issue-repeated-random-reviews-could-cancel-or-redirect-most-war-preparations-before-they-became-wars).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6051+) **War preparations against near-winning rivals no longer forget why the rival was chosen:** the same investigation found an AdvCiv-SAS integration bug in the victory-denial improvements from KI#184. China selected India on T237 with a war value of 320, including 300 because India threatened victory, but the inherited next-turn review omitted that urgency, saw only 17, and switched away. Reviews and alternative-target comparisons now preserve the victory-denial value; if an existing preparation later meets the same narrow distance, power, naval, and victory-timing limits used for emergency war selection, the AI can declare immediately instead of losing more turns. In the independent save-file 450 confirmation, this later direct-war path fired three times and all three declarations appeared in the game history, including wars against near-Space-victory rivals on T255 and T281. See [KI#189](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#189---fixedimproved-major-uwai-issue-repeated-random-reviews-could-cancel-or-redirect-most-war-preparations-before-they-became-wars) and [KI#184](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#184---fixedimproved-multiple-uwai-victory-denial-issues-could-let-imminent-space-winners-survive-no-war-before-fixing-this-regardless-of-whether-ai-was-very-strong-militarily-like-in-the-ramesses-run-or-very-weak-like-in-the-lincoln-run-then-even-after-the-initial-fix-wars-were-still-too-late-too-short-or-too-low-impact).

